## 7. The red gate — cited to D-024, not passed silently

D-024 requires that this verdict cite it rather than pass the red gate in
silence. It is cited here, and the citation is the ruling, not a reference.

**What I am passing the red gate on.** Not on the reds' order, which I ruled
defective in round 1 §5 and do not withdraw: all five were recorded against a
hand-stubbed tree that already held a verified-green implementation; the brief
prescribed skeleton-first in two explicit clauses; 01.log's green
"runTick gathers through opts.listModels / opts.versions" proves from inside
the artifacts that the tree was not the skeleton the spec described; the
bodies are weaker than the prescribed order would have produced in three
measured places; and nothing in the artifacts could have caught it. That
finding stands on the record unchanged, in ci/W-071-review-1.log, which the
opus links as its round-1 review evidence.

I am passing it on **`studio/decisions/D-024.md`**, the Patron's decree of
2026-09-25, which waives the red gate's ORDER for W-071 behaviours 1–5 only.

**Does D-024 satisfy the gate on its own terms? Yes, on all five of them.**

1. *Authority.* `by: patron`, `provenance: stated`. The Censor cannot accept a
   finding class or adopt a blocking-rule change alone (qa lex §4); the Patron
   is the only authority that can waive a standing rule. Correct authority,
   and the route I named in round 1 §7.
2. *Scope.* "The red gate's ORDER, for W-071 behaviours 1–5 only." Exactly
   coextensive with the finding — it waives the act's ordering, and touches
   neither the reds' bodies, nor their coverage, nor any other opus. It grants
   nothing I did not ask about.
3. *The act recorded plainly.* D-024 restates the mechanism in full
   (implemented, verified green, stubbed each piece to a no-op, recorded,
   restored) and my ruling in substance on all three of its limbs — the W-065
   05.log act, 01.log's gather assertions betraying the non-prescribed tree,
   and verifiability by candour rather than by design. Nothing is smoothed
   over; nothing is rewritten into compliance. It says "the defect is the
   unauthorized ACT, not the evidence's shape", which is precisely the
   distinction round 1 §5.6 drew between this case and W-047's.
4. *Kill condition.* `kill_when` names P-012's working-tree-identity
   mechanism — my own §5.7 proposal — and adds a second trigger I did not ask
   for and welcome: "or a second waiver of this class is requested before that
   mechanism lands, which is the pattern this decision exists to prevent".
   That makes the waiver self-limiting rather than a precedent. A waiver that
   can be granted twice is a rule change; this one cannot.
5. *Candour.* Credited "not as mitigation of the act, but as the difference
   between a waiver and a lesson in deception". That is the correct weight.
   The disclosure is why the ruling was possible; it is not why the act was
   permissible.

**A correction to my own round-1 log.** Round 1 §7 named `bisellium waive
<opus> --gate <id> --reason <text> --decision <id>` as a route. That was
wrong, and I record it rather than let it stand. `runWaive` writes
`status: waived` on a `kind: human` probatio only
(packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:760ff, and it refuses automated and agent
gates explicitly). This studio's probationes are tests/lint/types (automated)
and spec/review (agent) — there is no human gate, and the red gate is not a
probatio at all: reds are `ci/reds/<id>/NN.log`, checked by
`opus.red_evidence` / `opus.red_content` / `opus.red_sella` in
packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.ts, none of which inspects order or
working-tree state. So `waive` could never have targeted this gate. A decision
was not merely an acceptable instrument here — it was the ONLY available one,
which is itself part of why P-012's mechanism is needed.

**P-012, read as the petitio it is.** It states mechanism 1 (tree ancestry) as
considered and refuted, with W-071's five reds as the counterexample and my
reasoning verbatim: `dirty:<spec-gate-HEAD>` is byte-identical whether the
working tree held prescribed skeletons or stubbed-out finished code, because
the header names the committed HEAD and never the working tree's content. It
asks for mechanism 2 as an opus. `state: needs_you`, a legal value
(PETITIO_STATES, packages/schema/src/index.ts:174 — the fix at 4dbbf64 was
correct; `open` is not a wire value). The obligation is now the Patron's, and
D-024's kill_when is bound to it. Class C1 is therefore ADDRESSED for the
retro's Addressed ledger: target D-024 (formally accepted, with kill_when) and
P-012 (the mechanism that retires it).

## 8. Findings — round 2. None blocking.

A1 (advise) Duplicated autonomy predicate. tick.ts:493's
   `(c.autonomy ?? "L1") !== "L0"` is a second copy of computeDue's own filter
   (tick.ts:~150), ~340 lines apart in the same file. Two copies of one
   predicate is the drift the brief's own carve reasoning warns about, at
   smaller scale. A one-line shared helper closes it. Not worth a round.

A2 (note) The active path pays the catalog calls TWICE per tick. Measured: 7
   spawns on an active, unpaused, non-dry tick — 2 `codex debug models` and 2
   `codex --version`, because `tick` gathers for `computeDue` and then
   `probeBattery` gathers again for itself (tick's
   `runBattery({studio, now, only, maxTurns})` forwards no `listModels` /
   `versions`). Defensible: the battery stamps `harnessVersion` from a read
   taken at spend time (`liveVersionFor`, probe.ts:421), which is arguably the
   read that should be fresh. But it is two 5s-timeout listing calls per tick
   where one would do, and nobody has signed that. Design property, recorded
   so the next person to touch the seam sees it.

A3 (advise) W-071's record does not reach D-024. `traditio.next` still reads
   "red disposition awaits the Patron" — stale, it arrived — and names no
   decision, and no rule links an opus to the decision that waived one of its
   gates. A reader of studio/opera/W-071.md cannot find D-024 without grepping
   decisions/. Bookkeeping for the closing sella (and not mine to write).

A4 (note) A paused tick's health.json `due` no longer carries the probe item,
   since the paused branch computes it 3-arg. Semantically the better answer —
   while paused nothing is due to be acted on — and no reader is affected
   (apps/server/src/store.ts computes its own `due` and never reads this
   array). Recorded because it is a silent change to a generated record's
   contents that no assertion names.

Carried from round 1, unchanged and still not W-071's:
F4 `npm test` makes ~78 cold TLS connections to registry.npmjs.org from
   ci.test.ts's genuine `npm ci` fixture (c908a9f, untouched by this opus).
   Existing design, defensible, still an unsigned gate property.

## 9. Verdict: PASS

Round 1's two blocking findings are both closed, and closed at the root rather
than at the symptom:
  - F2 by moving the gather below the pause gate AND extending the same gate
    to all-L0 studios — my class C2's second instance, closed unasked —
    verified by my own sentinel measurement (0 spawns paused, 0 spawns L0,
    3 and 7 on the positive controls) and locked in by two mutations that each
    fail exactly the two assertions written for them.
  - F3 by rewriting behaviour 3's fixture on distinct primed ages with a
    dedicated control and no set fed forward, verified by the comparator
    mutation M1 — which round 1's fixture could not see and this one catches
    in four places.
The red gate passes on D-024, cited in §7 at length and on its own terms, with
the round-1 finding left standing on the record rather than withdrawn.
Gates green, certificates honest at tree:ce4dd826…, no novel finding class on
either officina, no red re-recorded, no scope creep, `probe.ts`'s seam edit
untouched.

Four advisories remain (§8), none of which warrants a round: A1 is a one-line
tidy, A2 and A4 are recorded design properties, A3 is bookkeeping for the
sella that closes the opus.

